First Author: Raji
Title: Computational methods for predicting the outcome of thoracic transplantation
Source number: 41  ￼

Very short summary: This study developed artificial neural network models for thoracic transplantation survival prediction using UNOS data. Five key findings were: (1) the original UNOS thoracic dataset contained 148,509 records and 543 attributes, from which a final analytic dataset of 485 adult cases and 24 input attributes was derived; (2) the primary proposed model was a multilayer perceptron (MLP) with back-propagation; (3) the MLP achieved reported accuracy of 97.1%; (4) a radial basis function (RBF) network was used as a secondary validation model and achieved 92.37% accuracy; and (5) in the comparison table, the proposed MLP also showed sensitivity 0.966 and specificity 0.984, outperforming the cited earlier heart–lung MLP system with 85.9% accuracy. Tables 1, 2, and 6 and Figures 1, 2, 3, and 6 were explicitly used for this extraction.

